and he is the former CTO of Enava.

14
00:01:01,670 --> 00:01:03,670
Owen wrote and contributed to several books,

15
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:11,990
and among them there is software system architecture, working with stakeholders using viewpoints and perspectives, with Nick Rosanski.

16
00:01:11,990 --> 00:01:16,630
and continuous architecture in practice, as I said before, with Murat and Pier Pere.

17
00:01:16,630 --> 00:01:24,070
I want to mention that Owen was also guest in episode 132 of the software engineer radio,

18
00:01:24,070 --> 00:01:26,149
and in that case was about top 10 architectural mistakes with Owen Woods.

19
00:01:26,159 --> 00:01:31,191
and this was published on the 16th of April 2009. Welcome to both.

20
00:01:31,191 --> 00:01:33,590
Is there anything that I miss that you would like to add?

21
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:35,670
No, those were great introductions. Thank you.

22
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:38,310
Okay, then let's get cracking.

23
00:01:38,310 --> 00:01:40,870
How do you define continuous architecture?

24
00:01:40,870 --> 00:01:45,350
the core of continuous architecture, if you go to the background.

25
00:01:45,350 --> 00:01:49,590
and just want to have a call out to Pierre who couldn't join us today.

26
00:01:49,590 --> 00:01:51,510
but Pierre and I, this is going back 10 years,

27
00:01:51,510 --> 00:01:56,710
we were thinking about we always called ourselves architects.

28
00:01:56,710 --> 00:02:01,270
and we wanted to combine what is good in architecture and what is good in agile.

29
00:02:01,270 --> 00:02:06,149
So the principle or the essence behind a continuous architecture is how do you make architecture relevant.

30
00:02:06,159 --> 00:02:08,229
and agile.

31
00:02:08,229 --> 00:02:11,510
but yeah the core of continuous architecture is the six principles,

32
00:02:11,510 --> 00:02:14,309
which we'll get into more detail as we speak.

33
00:02:14,309 --> 00:02:19,190
I noticed that you have things like architect products not just solutions,

34
00:02:19,190 --> 00:02:21,350
or architect for build test and deploy.

35
00:02:21,350 --> 00:02:26,470
So it seems that continuous architecture and compasses a bit more than the typical approaches.

36
00:02:26,470 --> 00:02:29,589
and definitions of software architecture. Am I correct?

37
00:02:29,599 --> 00:02:35,670
Well, I think continuous architecture focuses on getting the value delivered and empowering teams.

38
00:02:35,670 --> 00:02:38,869
The reason I think, cuz I didn't write it, but I've used it many times.

39
00:02:38,869 --> 00:02:41,830
The reason for the principle about build, test, and deploy is that if you don't,

40
00:02:41,830 --> 00:02:41,831
[clears throat]

41
00:02:41,831 --> 00:02:45,990
do those things, then the teams aren't empowered and value isn't delivered.

42
00:02:45,990 --> 00:02:53,270
So, it tries to take a slightly more holistic view of what software architecture work should encompass than. just structure of a software system.

43
00:02:53,280 --> 00:02:54,949
Yeah, I mean, I can add to that as well.

44
00:02:54,959 --> 00:03:01,030
I mean both Pierre and I worked in consultancy and in enterprises,

45
00:03:01,030 --> 00:03:04,390
and it's really about practicing architecture right.

46
00:03:04,390 --> 00:03:10,390
so it's not about theoretically what makes a good architecture, what makes architecture sensible in an enterprise right.

47
00:03:10,390 --> 00:03:13,190
so if you're just doing architecture maybe a very small company,

48
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:17,110
or you're building three or four people building a system,

49
00:03:17,110 --> 00:03:19,589
some of these things might not be as relevant.

50
00:03:19,589 --> 00:03:24,630
but it's really about practical architecture and what makes sense while you're building systems.

51
00:03:24,640 --> 00:03:30,790
And another thing which we might touch upon later is the while writing the first book, which is again 10 years ago.

52
00:03:30,799 --> 00:03:35,509
one question was asked to us like what's the unit of work of an architect,

53
00:03:35,509 --> 00:03:37,830
and we came to the conclusion at that time.

54
00:03:37,830 --> 00:03:39,990
we called it the architectural decision now,

55
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:47,350
this is before ADR's architectural decision records and other topics came about.

56
00:03:47,350 --> 00:03:52,470
but that really is, architecture is about the running software in production and making it sustainable,

57
00:03:52,470 --> 00:03:54,869
everything else doesn't matter.

58
00:03:54,869 --> 00:03:56,550
And what really matters are the decisions you make.

59
00:03:56,550 --> 00:04:00,470
that impact how that software running in production is constructed.

60
00:04:00,480 --> 00:04:02,630
Okay.

61
00:04:02,630 --> 00:04:04,550
And now maybe we can go in more detail into the principles.

62
00:04:04,550 --> 00:04:06,949
I know that there are six of them.

63
00:04:06,949 --> 00:04:09,190
Can you briefly explain those to us?

64
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:11,110
The first one is quite fundamental.

65
00:04:11,110 --> 00:04:16,550
It's about thinking about a productled approach as opposed to a projectled approach.

66
00:04:16,550 --> 00:04:19,910
And today this sounds entirely obvious, and I sometimes do stand in front of audiences. who look at me and go.

67
00:04:19,919 --> 00:04:26,469
and you say well you got to remember these things were being thought out in 2011 1213.

68
00:04:26,469 --> 00:04:28,230
I ask the audience for those who are old enough,

69
00:04:28,230 --> 00:04:33,189
how many of you in 2012 were thinking about building products inside enterprises as opposed to running projects.

70
00:04:33,189 --> 00:04:35,430
and very few hands go up.

71
00:04:35,430 --> 00:04:38,870
It's more a reminder that actually if you take a product oriented approach.

72
00:04:38,870 --> 00:04:41,189
and you think about the long-term planning.

73
00:04:41,189 --> 00:04:44,790
and a product inside an organization that has a particular collection of stakeholders.

74
00:04:44,790 --> 00:04:48,950
and will have a long-term life, and so needs to be managed in a sustainable way.

75
00:04:48,950 --> 00:04:52,390
It drives you to thinking about all your work as building products.

76
00:04:52,390 --> 00:04:57,590
Even if they're never sold outside the organization, even if there's only one copy, it's still a product.

77
00:04:57,590 --> 00:04:59,189
The second principle is really fundamental to architecture in general.

78
00:04:59,199 --> 00:05:04,070
It's about focusing on quality attributes. Most software architects do obsess about quality attributes.

79
00:05:04,070 --> 00:05:08,710
But especially when you're introducing these ideas to people. It's actually not obvious to people.

80
00:05:08,710 --> 00:05:10,310
They think architecture is about boxes and lines.

81
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:11,909
It's about structure.

82
00:05:11,909 --> 00:05:15,909
The only reason for the structure is to achieve quality attributes.

83
00:05:15,909 --> 00:05:18,390
Security, performance, scalability, resilience, and maintainability.

84
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:23,350
This is actually an interesting point, because often in many software products.

85
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:27,430
or projects there is a lot of focus on features.

86
00:05:27,430 --> 00:05:29,430
and then the quality attributes are often an afterthought.

87
00:05:29,440 --> 00:05:32,469
Exactly. and that's why it's such an important principle.

88
00:05:32,469 --> 00:05:35,909
It's that the quality attributes have to be lived and breathed by the team.

89
00:05:35,909 --> 00:05:40,310
You don't want someone over here called an architect who it's a problem.

90
00:05:40,310 --> 00:05:44,150
And generally speaking, I'd say with all due respect to high performing scrum teams,

91
00:05:44,150 --> 00:05:47,350
they can be doing a great job working with their product owner delivering features.

92
00:05:47,350 --> 00:05:51,830
They find it very hard to step back and take that more holistic view, unless you remind them to.

93
00:05:51,830 --> 00:05:55,189
because after all, security is not something that one team can actually achieve.

94
00:05:55,189 --> 00:05:58,390
They need an environment that they plug into to achieve security.

95
00:05:58,390 --> 00:06:01,670
And so that's why it's an architectural principle to focus on quality attributes,

96
00:06:01,670 --> 00:06:04,550
because it's a holistic systemic characteristic.

97
00:06:04,550 --> 00:06:08,550
The third principle, I suspect, because again I didn't write it, but we can ask Mur.

98
00:06:08,550 --> 00:06:12,390
I suspect came direct from really the agile manifesto type thinking,

99
00:06:12,390 --> 00:06:14,230
which is delay decisions as long as you can.

100
00:06:14,230 --> 00:06:17,430
This is a reaction to the kind of architecture that I used to do.

101
00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:18,950
Absolutely.

102
00:06:18,950 --> 00:06:20,790
If you look at the software systems architecture book,

103
00:06:20,790 --> 00:06:24,629
I put the graph from that showing architecture activity over time up on slides now.

104
00:06:24,629 --> 00:06:26,230
so I can laugh at it, because it's my graph.

105
00:06:26,240 --> 00:06:29,270
where we used to say well of course architecture.

106
00:06:29,270 --> 00:06:31,110
lots and lots of decisions up front to de-risk the whole enterprise.

107
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:37,830
and then we have some decisions at the end where we realize that now we're getting towards production, we need to rethink a few things.

108
00:06:37,830 --> 00:06:39,909
Of course, that's a pretty flawed approach.

109
00:06:39,909 --> 00:06:44,629
Actually, as we discovered over the years, some decisions actually do need to be made very early.

110
00:06:44,629 --> 00:06:48,070
And there are contexts where actually more decisions need to be made early.

111
00:06:48,070 --> 00:06:49,990
But the earlier you make a decision, the less information you have,

112
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:53,189
therefore the poorer decision you probably make.

113
00:06:53,189 --> 00:06:55,029
We need to make the decision as late as we can.

114
00:06:55,029 --> 00:06:57,749
As the agile communities have been telling us for is it 25 years now, Jiovali?

115
00:06:57,759 --> 00:06:59,909
Yeah. To make the decision as late as possible,

116
00:06:59,919 --> 00:07:03,270
because that gives you the most accurate context to make the decision.

117
00:07:03,270 --> 00:07:10,230
This is actually a good point, because well I worked in many projects and funnily enough.

118
00:07:10,230 --> 00:07:15,189
often when people go with early decisions with grand designs of how the system should be.

119
00:07:15,189 --> 00:07:17,350
and trying to overthink the evolution and everything.

120
00:07:17,350 --> 00:07:22,550
what happens is that the systems have this horrible habit of evolving in a direction.

121
00:07:22,550 --> 00:07:25,430
that is different from the one you forecasted.

122
00:07:25,430 --> 00:07:28,870
So you end up to have to maintain a lot of stuff that you don't need.

123
00:07:28,870 --> 00:07:33,909
because otherwise you cannot even compile the code plus hack into the system the things.

124
00:07:33,909 --> 00:07:36,629
that will support the new direction it is going to towards.

125
00:07:36,629 --> 00:07:41,749
So you may end up with a spaghetti monster that you really didn't think of at the beginning.

126
00:07:41,759 --> 00:07:43,670
Exactly.

127
00:07:43,670 --> 00:07:49,830
The number of people over the last 15 years who've told me I just want a futureproof architecture.

128
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:54,470
pause for a moment before breaking the bad news to them. There's no such thing.

129
00:07:54,479 --> 00:07:58,390
There's just the simplest possible effective architecture for now,

130
00:07:58,390 --> 00:08:01,830
and we'll maximize the options for change later by keeping it simple.

131
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:05,350
Now, before letting you proceed, I can tell you this.

132
00:08:05,350 --> 00:08:08,950
that this is a real thing that happened to me in a requirement document.

133
00:08:08,950 --> 00:08:13,670
a requirement that was written that the architecture has to be flexible to support future requirements.

134
00:08:13,670 --> 00:08:15,589
And I explained the person at the time.

135
00:08:15,589 --> 00:08:21,749
that I didn't have a crystal ball to actually be able to do that in advance.

136
00:08:21,749 --> 00:08:24,390
Yeah, I'll give to a few examples about this.

137
00:08:24,400 --> 00:08:28,790
For example, I mean one experience I had was people were designing the system.

138
00:08:28,790 --> 00:08:32,630
and they were expecting thousands and thousands of users.

139
00:08:32,630 --> 00:08:35,029
So they built a lot of complex capabilities in the system,

140
00:08:35,039 --> 00:08:39,029
but it went live and it never reached that user capacity required.

141
00:08:39,029 --> 00:08:42,070
So all that effort of building that was wasted.

142
00:08:42,070 --> 00:08:46,230
And back to your flexibility comment, Giovania,

143
00:08:46,230 --> 00:08:50,070
another thing at least maybe used happen quite a lot when rules engines were very popular.

144
00:08:50,070 --> 00:08:53,350
You would say, "Oh, I need the system to be flexible.

145
00:08:53,350 --> 00:08:57,431
That means I'll just write rules and I'll put a rules engine on my system. and I'll just change the rules."

146
00:08:57,440 --> 00:09:03,269
Which usually didn't work as anticipated, and you just put a rules engine while not required.

147
00:09:03,269 --> 00:09:07,030
Well, you could just get by with just some simple logic. So it's those type of things that.

148
00:09:07,030 --> 00:09:11,030
if you're thinking you have the most future proof architecture as Owen said.

149
00:09:11,030 --> 00:09:14,870
and you try to build it, you'll just waste a lot of time and effort.

150
00:09:14,870 --> 00:09:18,949
and probably all architectural decisions about you balance different quality attributes.

151
00:09:18,949 --> 00:09:23,670
and because you build a complex system you might overlook some other quality attributes. that you might not have thought of.

152
00:09:23,680 --> 00:09:26,390
Yeah. And one final comment on this one,

153
00:09:26,399 --> 00:09:33,350
presented this starchex a lot of people understand it as do not make design decisions.

154
00:09:33,350 --> 00:09:35,030
and that is not the intent at all right,

155
00:09:35,030 --> 00:09:37,030
we want to make design decisions as I said before,

156
00:09:37,030 --> 00:09:38,870
it is the unit of work of an architect.

157
00:09:38,870 --> 00:09:43,590
if you do nothing else you just make sure you know which design decisions you're making.

158
00:09:43,590 --> 00:09:49,350
so it's about making them in the right context as Owen said yeah.

159
00:09:49,350 --> 00:09:51,829
so to recap the first three, build products or projects,

160
00:09:51,829 --> 00:09:55,990
quality attributes over functional requirements, delay decisions as long as you can.

161
00:09:55,990 --> 00:10:00,470
And then the fourth one is focus on build, test, deploy, and operate.

162
00:10:00,470 --> 00:10:04,070
I think this again is not about only focusing on build, test, deploy,

163
00:10:04,070 --> 00:10:07,590
and operate, but it's making sure that someone is thinking about build, test, deploy, and operate.

164
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:10,949
Again, guessing a little bit reading between the lines.

165
00:10:10,949 --> 00:10:18,150
Historically, software architects perhaps been quite good at debating design patterns and software structures and code line organization.

166
00:10:18,150 --> 00:10:20,870
and how the unit test should run,

167
00:10:20,870 --> 00:10:26,150
and actually have then left a yawning chasm between that and how code runs in operation.

168
00:10:26,150 --> 00:10:27,670
I have a former colleague Edward Butler.

169
00:10:27,670 --> 00:10:32,389
who I worked with quite a while in Dava who has a a great turn of phrase.

170
00:10:32,389 --> 00:10:33,910
and Ed started really on the op side of the house.

171
00:10:33,910 --> 00:10:35,430
and then moved to the dev side of the house.

172
00:10:35,440 --> 00:10:39,110
So he for a period was very influential in DevOps as you might imagine.

173
00:10:39,110 --> 00:10:42,230
and he was one of the pioneers and he had a lovely phrase.

174
00:10:42,230 --> 00:10:45,269
He said on the one side you've got operations, on the other side you've got development.

175
00:10:45,269 --> 00:10:49,509
and in between you have the desert of responsibility where nobody wants to tread,

176
00:10:49,509 --> 00:10:51,269
because they think they're going to die.

177
00:10:51,269 --> 00:10:57,509
and architecting for build test deploy operate is about building the bridge over the desert.

178
00:10:57,509 --> 00:11:01,910
so that actually code can stop just being beautiful structures and can actually start delivering value in production.

179
00:11:01,920 --> 00:11:05,590
Yeah to me actually this principle is something that is quite interesting,

180
00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:07,509
because I think is an excellent one.

181
00:11:07,509 --> 00:11:12,550
It also is something that really when you talk about architectures often with architects,

182
00:11:12,550 --> 00:11:14,949
they don't really emphasize the architects for build test and deploy.

183
00:11:14,959 --> 00:11:21,430
often there is the emphasis on the boxes and lines if you like yeah, I'm simplifying a bit.

184
00:11:21,430 --> 00:11:27,269
but it's often on this on the structure usually on the static behavior, sometimes some sequence diagrams.

185
00:11:27,269 --> 00:11:29,829
but then how are we going to test this.

186
00:11:29,829 --> 00:11:31,829
or how are we going to build the pretty stuff is never discussed.

187
00:11:31,839 --> 00:11:39,030
and I think historically there were reasons for that, not maybe brilliant reasons but there were practical reasons.

188
00:11:39,030 --> 00:11:41,430
I grew up in a product development,

189
00:11:41,430 --> 00:11:44,710
and there I mean the development team software engineers didn't deploy anything.

190
00:11:44,710 --> 00:11:48,949
We had to produce a binary that came out of a build cycle.

191
00:11:48,949 --> 00:11:51,590
that went to show you my age.

192
00:11:51,590 --> 00:11:54,949
Something known as the gold master, meaning cuz the first CDs were always gold.

193
00:11:54,949 --> 00:11:57,030
so you could tell them apart from the other CDs.

194
00:11:57,030 --> 00:11:58,949
So it was stamped the gold master that went to the test team.

195
00:11:58,959 --> 00:12:03,430
And actually that was sort of sane and sensible. This wasn't a cloud deployment.

196
00:12:03,430 --> 00:12:04,949
This was going to have to go to a customer.

197
00:12:04,949 --> 00:12:06,470
the customer was literally going to put.

198
00:12:06,470 --> 00:12:11,509
that CD into a server machine and try and install the product. So the fact that you had a separate team with the skills,

199
00:12:11,509 --> 00:12:14,870
the customer had checking what was coming out of the engineering build cycle.

200
00:12:14,880 --> 00:12:16,550
actually made a lot of sense.

201
00:12:16,550 --> 00:12:19,910
But as soon as you start operating your own software as a SAS vendor.

202
00:12:19,910 --> 00:12:21,670
that suddenly doesn't make any sense anymore.

203
00:12:21,670 --> 00:12:24,310
And I think a lot of big companies enterprises were set up that way too.

204
00:12:24,310 --> 00:12:30,150
Do you remember they used to have no, well many still do, quiet isolated ops function.

205
00:12:30,150 --> 00:12:33,910
Dev are not allowed to have any access or control over production at all.

206
00:12:33,910 --> 00:12:35,829
They can fiddle in their test environments,

207
00:12:35,829 --> 00:12:37,829
but there's not a very serious attempt made to sync them up.

208
00:12:37,839 --> 00:12:44,069
And again, something that looks a bit like a gold master comes out of development.

209
00:12:44,069 --> 00:12:46,470
and is handed to ops across the desert of responsibility.

210
00:12:46,470 --> 00:12:49,829
Ops try and install it, tell you it doesn't work, and hand it back again.

211
00:12:49,829 --> 00:12:54,230
Or even worse, boot it and then hope for the best in full knowledge.

212
00:12:54,230 --> 00:12:56,230
that it'll probably go wrong.

213
00:12:56,230 --> 00:13:00,870
and then have to deal with the fallout at half past 2 in the morning. when some other region starts using it.

214
00:13:00,870 --> 00:13:02,629
and they suddenly discover they haven't got their languages loaded.

215
00:13:02,629 --> 00:13:04,470
all whatever gross error has been made.

216
00:13:04,480 --> 00:13:08,870
Also the test bit is actually quite interesting,

217
00:13:08,870 --> 00:13:12,790
because in practice you can have something that that high level architecture two systems look identical.

218
00:13:12,800 --> 00:13:14,389
from a high level point of view.

219
00:13:14,389 --> 00:13:18,870
same module same things, but one is testable the other is not.

220
00:13:18,870 --> 00:13:22,230
simply because you didn't think about how are you going to test this thing.

221
00:13:22,240 --> 00:13:25,590
Yeah. So to me also this is quite an important point,

222
00:13:25,590 --> 00:13:28,710
another one that is often not discussed even nowadays.

223
00:13:28,710 --> 00:13:30,470
No, no, I totally agree.

224
00:13:30,470 --> 00:13:35,190
But I think it's all about back to what we said about the principles about the team.

225
00:13:35,190 --> 00:13:37,509
