So it's not that architects are not valuable anymore.

452
00:27:09,830 --> 00:27:14,950
Their relationship with the teams are different from being somewhere else designing things.

453
00:27:14,960 --> 00:27:17,110
Exactly.

454
00:27:17,110 --> 00:27:21,269
I mean it's actually to make architecture valuable is the intent, right?

455
00:27:21,269 --> 00:27:25,669
And I think in lots of traditionally the ivory tower architect gets a bad name in organizations.

456
00:27:25,669 --> 00:27:30,390
because you have set up separate architecture teams.

457
00:27:30,390 --> 00:27:35,190
that dip in and dip out of different product teams or project teams.

458
00:27:35,190 --> 00:27:37,590
And the idea is bringing accountability back to the team, right?

459
00:27:37,600 --> 00:27:42,789
because the unit of work are architectural decisions and the team makes those architectural decisions.

460
00:27:42,789 --> 00:27:49,029
So anyone who contributes to that architectural decision is you could say an architect.

461
00:27:49,029 --> 00:27:56,231
That doesn't mean you don't need central teams to set wider principles or wider guidelines for the organization. They need to exist.

462
00:27:56,231 --> 00:28:02,870
But it's to separate this thing of like having architects on one side are the only people.

463
00:28:02,870 --> 00:28:04,950
that make decisions and determine what's good or what's bad.

464
00:28:04,950 --> 00:28:09,591
and everyone else as just toiling writing code. And you can take different views.

465
00:28:09,600 --> 00:28:12,310
You can say the architects see them as people.

466
00:28:12,310 --> 00:28:16,389
who just don't know what they're doing and make wrong all the wrong decisions.

467
00:28:16,389 --> 00:28:20,310
or the people on the ground actually working to develop the software look at the architects.

468
00:28:20,310 --> 00:28:22,310
and say who are these guys.

469
00:28:22,310 --> 00:28:24,149
they have no idea of our software of our codes.

470
00:28:24,149 --> 00:28:26,710
and they just come here with they pontificate.

471
00:28:26,710 --> 00:28:30,630
So we really want to bring that together and make architecture relevant.

472
00:28:30,630 --> 00:28:34,710
rather than having two separate focus areas in the organization trying to do architecture.

473
00:28:34,720 --> 00:28:38,389
Yeah. Another question around the decisions.

474
00:28:38,389 --> 00:28:43,830
As we mentioned before, the architectural decisions are kind of the say what architects do.

475
00:28:43,830 --> 00:28:48,470
is that the main output of the work in a continuous architecture context?

476
00:28:48,470 --> 00:28:50,630
How do you record them and maintain those decisions?

477
00:28:50,640 --> 00:28:53,909
Because if it was waterfall, everything they said up front,

478
00:28:53,909 --> 00:29:00,149
you know, I have all my decisions written, agreed, signed in blood, and that's it. I don't need to do much.

479
00:29:00,149 --> 00:29:04,871
Well, written, agreed, signed, ignored, ignored. That's the the full life cycle.

480
00:29:04,880 --> 00:29:08,549
But in a continuous architecture situation, especially.

481
00:29:08,549 --> 00:29:12,630
when we work start from small and evolve the system and take decisions all the time.

482
00:29:12,630 --> 00:29:15,830
we need to record these decisions and manage them in a bit of a different way.

483
00:29:15,830 --> 00:29:18,630
It's much more dynamic, isn't it?

484
00:29:18,630 --> 00:29:22,470
So how do you do that without actually being overwhelmed by the process?

485
00:29:22,480 --> 00:29:24,710
It's quite difficult.

486
00:29:24,710 --> 00:29:26,310
And interestingly, in the last two to three years,

487
00:29:26,310 --> 00:29:31,910
I've worked with a couple of clients who were actively trying to figure out how to do this. They were very well-intentioned.

488
00:29:31,919 --> 00:29:34,311
Just a sec. They met ADRs.

489
00:29:34,311 --> 00:29:36,070
They sort of unleashed ADRs across their team.

490
00:29:36,080 --> 00:29:38,230
Just a sec.

491
00:29:38,230 --> 00:29:39,750
So ADRs we mean sorry architecture decision records.

492
00:29:39,760 --> 00:29:41,431
Yeah. They picked a format.

493
00:29:41,431 --> 00:29:43,909
They did some thinking about how they wanted theirs to work.

494
00:29:43,909 --> 00:29:50,389
They started producing architectural decision records almost not exactly ad hoc but you know very continuously.

495
00:29:50,389 --> 00:29:54,149
And they ended up with this huge heap of architecture decision records,

496
00:29:54,149 --> 00:29:55,669
and it wasn't quite clear what to do with it.

497
00:29:55,669 --> 00:29:57,750
And I think the lesson I learned after two.

498
00:29:57,750 --> 00:29:59,350
or three times of kind of trying to help people with this is.

499
00:29:59,360 --> 00:30:06,789
it's one very important to work out what you're going to do with your decision records.

500
00:30:06,789 --> 00:30:11,510
And secondly, you need to put some effort into curating your decision records very early.

501
00:30:11,510 --> 00:30:16,870
Otherwise, they become a sort of documentation debt, because you don't know which ones are valid.

502
00:30:16,870 --> 00:30:19,830
Some of them have probably now been retired, but they're still sitting there in the overall heap.

503
00:30:19,830 --> 00:30:25,029
It's very hard to find a specific decision, because there's just they're very loosely organized.

504
00:30:25,029 --> 00:30:26,549
The best solution I found for that,

505
00:30:26,549 --> 00:30:28,389
I didn't find a great solution but the best solution was using wikis.

506
00:30:28,399 --> 00:30:32,950
because they can be hierarchical along with rich content tagging.

507
00:30:32,950 --> 00:30:37,430
and so you can use I forget all the the right content term for it,

508
00:30:37,430 --> 00:30:41,909
but you can effectively have a web of content linked together by the tags.

509
00:30:41,909 --> 00:30:43,510
with an overall primary organizing hierarchy.

510
00:30:43,510 --> 00:30:45,190
so you can go and find stuff quickly.

511
00:30:45,190 --> 00:30:46,870
and find related things quickly and you link between the records.

512
00:30:46,870 --> 00:30:50,149
but the thing that you have to keep persuading people to do even.

513
00:30:50,149 --> 00:30:52,070
though they're busy even though they've got three jobs is.

514
00:30:52,080 --> 00:30:55,590
when they hit a decision that that looks wrong,

515
00:30:55,590 --> 00:30:57,269
the documentation looks wrong, flag it up and do something about it.

516
00:30:57,279 --> 00:31:04,630
And secondly, on a pretty routine basis, review decisions in the overall decision base.

517
00:31:04,630 --> 00:31:08,870
I'd be retiring the ones that should be retired, correct ones that are incorrect.

518
00:31:08,870 --> 00:31:10,470
or at least flag things up that are inconsistent.

519
00:31:10,470 --> 00:31:13,110
and particularly if they're not consistent with the principles,

520
00:31:13,110 --> 00:31:14,870
because the point of the principles is to guide the decisions.

521
00:31:14,880 --> 00:31:19,430
That's a really you talking about feedback loops, that's a great feedback loop.

522
00:31:19,430 --> 00:31:23,430
So are we ignoring the principles when we make decisions, and so the decisions aren't great decisions?

523
00:31:23,430 --> 00:31:28,710
or are we making good decisions deliberately violating the principles because the principles aren't right.

524
00:31:28,710 --> 00:31:32,389
and so you need to go back and think well these principles don't reflect our priorities.

525
00:31:32,399 --> 00:31:39,830
I mean there's I think two sides to like like there's the managing decisions as part of the.

526
00:31:39,830 --> 00:31:41,990
process almost as implementing principle three.

527
00:31:41,990 --> 00:31:44,789
is one way to look at it.

528
00:31:44,789 --> 00:31:47,110
and then how you curate decisions after you've made them.

529
00:31:47,110 --> 00:31:51,029
so that you have a proper knowledge base is another way to look at it right.

530
00:31:51,029 --> 00:31:53,029
and and they I think they're both complimentary.

531
00:31:53,039 --> 00:31:56,149
but you know talked about good ways of how you can make sure.

532
00:31:56,149 --> 00:31:59,990
that your collections of decisions are already there.

533
00:31:59,990 --> 00:32:02,230
so you understand why the hell the software was built in the way it does.

534
00:32:02,240 --> 00:32:09,190
but also I've found using like a basic camb board of architectural decisions on a product.

535
00:32:09,190 --> 00:32:12,470
and you just go through them and you have decisions as I said. that you're not going to make yet.

536
00:32:12,470 --> 00:32:15,830
you have decisions you've made of course once you make a decision.

537
00:32:15,830 --> 00:32:20,710
then it goes into the heap of you know how you you want to review them etc.

538
00:32:20,710 --> 00:32:24,871
but that can bound board thing really really helps focus the team on just saying you know what. are the.

539
00:32:24,871 --> 00:32:29,590
and that's the process of making the decisions and implementing principle three,

540
00:32:29,590 --> 00:32:32,870
I think that's just as important as the post the decision is curation.

541
00:32:32,880 --> 00:32:34,789
Yeah, in some projects where we work.

542
00:32:34,799 --> 00:32:40,950
actually we found useful to put many decisions in the same configuration management repository of the code.

543
00:32:40,950 --> 00:32:43,190
that was affected.

544
00:32:43,190 --> 00:32:44,950
but then the decisions of higher level maybe in wikis.

545
00:32:44,950 --> 00:32:46,870
or somewhere else basically a mixed kind of approach.

546
00:32:46,880 --> 00:32:52,389
because the reason is sometimes when you even.

547
00:32:52,389 --> 00:32:55,990
if you are in a continuous delivery approach sometimes you have a version in production of a system.

548
00:32:55,990 --> 00:32:58,470
and you want to know what the decisions related to.

549
00:32:58,470 --> 00:33:02,710
that thing not necessarily the new decisions for what you're working on at the moment.

550
00:33:02,720 --> 00:33:04,630
But it's really a complicated thing.

551
00:33:04,630 --> 00:33:09,350
But it definitely requires some effort from the company to actually address the problem.

552
00:33:09,360 --> 00:33:10,950
Yeah.

553
00:33:10,950 --> 00:33:13,190
And very dependent on the culture of the organization as well.

554
00:33:13,190 --> 00:33:19,270
I mean I mean continuous architecture has principles and recommendations but it's not a you know a cookbook. Yeah.

555
00:33:19,279 --> 00:33:25,830
You know in the sense that we don't tell you mix you know two teaspoons of decisions with.

556
00:33:25,830 --> 00:33:27,509
one tablespoon of quality attributes.

557
00:33:27,509 --> 00:33:29,909
and you know you get the perfect system.

558
00:33:29,909 --> 00:33:32,310
It's just ways to think about how you do architecture.

559
00:33:32,310 --> 00:33:34,950
Now moving on a bit about validation.

560
00:33:34,950 --> 00:33:38,950
How do we validate an architecture in a continuous architecture context?

561
00:33:38,960 --> 00:33:42,470
Well, the feedback loops are quite fundamental to it.

562
00:33:42,470 --> 00:33:47,111
Um, there's nothing to stop you though doing what you might call traditional architectural evaluation on a system. that's, you know.

563
00:33:47,120 --> 00:33:53,750
using a scenario-based technique like ATM or something bit lighter weight and less formal.

564
00:33:53,750 --> 00:33:56,310
but you still understand the overall architecture.

565
00:33:56,310 --> 00:34:00,070
Um, so you you can still do what you might call an abstract evaluation of it.

566
00:34:00,070 --> 00:34:05,029
But I think the key evaluation is how well does it work, and that's what your feedback loop should be telling you.

567
00:34:05,039 --> 00:34:07,430
Yeah.

568
00:34:07,430 --> 00:34:11,349
The end goal is to as we said like you're accountable for the system that's running in production.

569
00:34:11,349 --> 00:34:14,149
So if you really think about it.

570
00:34:14,149 --> 00:34:16,629
if you know you how well the system is operating plus how.

571
00:34:16,629 --> 00:34:18,869
well you can maintain it over time.

572
00:34:18,869 --> 00:34:25,349
because architecture is about building a sustainable system over time, those two things validated.

573
00:34:25,349 --> 00:34:30,069
but yeah I'm known as right there are lots of techniques for validating an architecture.

574
00:34:30,069 --> 00:34:33,589
and there's you know and you could do architecture reviews.

575
00:34:33,589 --> 00:34:35,510
or you could implement ATM you know all those things are valid.

576
00:34:35,510 --> 00:34:39,669
we're not very, but this I'm not very prescriptive on that whatever works.

577
00:34:39,669 --> 00:34:44,310
I I'll tell you an interesting way we validated architecture designs years ago,

578
00:34:44,320 --> 00:34:48,790
and it was not my idea it was my boss's idea.

579
00:34:48,790 --> 00:34:53,030
was actually we bought huge big Lego blocks like the ones that you play with.

580
00:34:53,030 --> 00:34:55,669
and we had sessions where people would describe their architecture,

581
00:34:55,669 --> 00:35:01,349
you know, here's the database, here's the web server, here's that component or that other component.

582
00:35:01,349 --> 00:35:03,190
and they try to build it with Lego blocks on the floor,

583
00:35:03,200 --> 00:35:07,031
and then people would walk around and ask questions. What if I move this?

584
00:35:07,031 --> 00:35:12,390
So, if this component, you know, you could just actually physically kick a Lego block and say,

585
00:35:12,390 --> 00:35:14,790
if this component goes down, what goes on?

586
00:35:14,790 --> 00:35:19,510
So I mean again it's not a very formal technique but it's you know.

587
00:35:19,510 --> 00:35:21,990
I think there's multiple ways but the end objective is.

588
00:35:21,990 --> 00:35:25,510
if the system is working and you can maintain it over time then you're successful.

589
00:35:25,520 --> 00:35:29,349
Basically in short the you know you can exploit the feedback loop.

590
00:35:29,349 --> 00:35:33,670
but also you can apply some for the lack of a better wording more traditional techniques,

591
00:35:33,670 --> 00:35:37,751
and they still work in the context of continuous architecture. Am I correct?

592
00:35:37,760 --> 00:35:43,910
as long as you understand your system. But to be fair, techniques like ATM which are often thought of as very formal and documentationdriven,

593
00:35:43,910 --> 00:35:46,150
they're not at all.

594
00:35:46,150 --> 00:35:49,750
When they designed ATAM, they would typically turn up at a US Department of Defense subcontractor.

595
00:35:49,760 --> 00:35:52,550
because that's was the market it was built for.

596
00:35:52,550 --> 00:35:55,510
And the first thing they'd say is where's all the architecture documentation.

597
00:35:55,510 --> 00:35:58,790
and this large defense subcontractor who'd spent a billion dollars would go,

598
00:35:58,790 --> 00:36:00,470
"Oh, we haven't got any of that."

599
00:36:00,470 --> 00:36:02,230
So the first step of ATAB is like work out how the system works.

600
00:36:02,240 --> 00:36:04,710
So hopefully if you've taken a continuous approach,

601
00:36:04,710 --> 00:36:06,630
you've got all your decisions, you got minimal models,

602
00:36:06,630 --> 00:36:09,750
you got your principles, you actually know how your system works.

603
00:36:09,750 --> 00:36:11,910
So actually assessing it shouldn't be a huge problem.

604
00:36:11,920 --> 00:36:13,750
Okay?

605
00:36:13,750 --> 00:36:15,670
So as long as you know what the system does, it should be fine.

606
00:36:15,680 --> 00:36:18,069
Should be fine in terms instead of governance.

607
00:36:18,069 --> 00:36:24,151
So making sure that the architectural decisions are implemented and implemented correctly. Yeah.

608
00:36:24,151 --> 00:36:27,750
How do you approach governance from a continuous architectural perspective?

609
00:36:27,760 --> 00:36:29,349
Governance, right?

610
00:36:29,349 --> 00:36:31,670
We wrote about that in the first book.

611
00:36:31,670 --> 00:36:33,829
you know, we had a chapter or part of a chapter on it.

612
00:36:33,829 --> 00:36:36,470
And the whole idea is that, you know,

613
00:36:36,470 --> 00:36:41,349
again, we're talking about the team and the team owning the architecture of their system.

614
00:36:41,349 --> 00:36:44,550
and there's lots of decisions that they need to make. So when you think about governance, you want to make.

615
00:36:44,560 --> 00:36:50,790
sure you enable the teams to be able to make those decisions as quickly and explicitly as possible.

616
00:36:50,790 --> 00:36:54,310
But at the same time, you want certain enterprise standards.

617
00:36:54,310 --> 00:36:57,030
It could be, you know, database technologies.

618
00:36:57,030 --> 00:36:59,349
It could be certain techniques for logging.

619
00:36:59,349 --> 00:37:03,750
or certain you know metadata you want captured at certain events etc.

620
00:37:03,750 --> 00:37:05,430
So the whole idea about governance is.

621
00:37:05,430 --> 00:37:11,030
that you have you make very few but significant decisions at the top of the corporation.

622
00:37:11,040 --> 00:37:15,349
by the I guess the most senior architecture governance body you have.

623
00:37:15,359 --> 00:37:20,950
and then depending on the size of the organization you can get to divisional level and lower levels.

624
00:37:20,950 --> 00:37:24,470
but the majority of architectural decisions are still made by the team,

625
00:37:24,470 --> 00:37:27,510
but they make it within the context of that.

626
00:37:27,510 --> 00:37:31,349
Now for that to work you need to make sure that the communication is very effective.

627
00:37:31,349 --> 00:37:36,870
You need to make sure the enterprise standards are really communicated to all the teams as effectively,

628
00:37:36,870 --> 00:37:41,270
which is difficult on its own but somewhat achievable.

629
00:37:41,270 --> 00:37:44,950
but what I think we lack as an industry and I don't know we even cracked this.

630
00:37:44,950 --> 00:37:51,109
how you make sure that the decisions made by the teams are visible to other layers in the.

631
00:37:51,109 --> 00:37:53,030
organization and to other teams right.

632
00:37:53,030 --> 00:37:57,589
I think that knowledge management of architectural decisions is still a huge gap.

633
00:37:57,589 --> 00:38:00,790
and eventually what happens is that something goes wrong.

634
00:38:00,800 --> 00:38:06,870
Someone senior is called dragged him what's called the senior person accountable representatives.

635
00:38:06,870 --> 00:38:10,790
and dragged in front of people to say well this system went down.

636
00:38:10,790 --> 00:38:17,750
and you know who approved XY Z, and they don't know because they they don't have that transparency.

637
00:38:17,750 --> 00:38:20,950
and after that happens then they start becoming much more strict they say.

638
00:38:20,950 --> 00:38:22,870
okay now I want every architectural decision approved by me.

639
00:38:22,880 --> 00:38:27,750
you know and this could impact tens of teams right.

640
00:38:27,750 --> 00:38:31,109
so the I think the trick in governance is again I.

641
00:38:31,109 --> 00:38:32,710
If the unit of work is the architectural decision,

642
00:38:32,710 --> 00:38:39,589
you want to make that in context of bigger principles and rules set by the organization.

643
00:38:39,589 --> 00:38:44,470
But you need transparency and cracking that transparency not I think is is a big challenge.

644
00:38:44,470 --> 00:38:46,470
but you know it takes a lot of time.

645
00:38:46,470 --> 00:38:48,630
It's a big knowledge management effort.

646
00:38:48,630 --> 00:38:50,630
and you know most people who are technologists.

647
00:38:50,630 --> 00:38:56,790
and want to do architecture and things like that sometimes don't want to you know focus on that.

648
00:38:56,790 --> 00:38:59,670
I guess the more social and knowledge sharing aspects of developing software.

649
00:38:59,680 --> 00:39:05,670
and are there any elements of the governance that can be actually automated.

650
00:39:05,670 --> 00:39:11,190
you know something that maybe can end up in the build pipelines or some level of automated checks.

651
00:39:11,200 --> 00:39:20,069
there's quite nice support in open source libraries like arunit for checking some quite sophisticated dependency in code.

652
00:39:20,069 --> 00:39:24,630
structure implementation of patterns those kind of constraints in in code.

653
00:39:24,630 --> 00:39:27,030
but that's a very microlevel architecture.

654
00:39:27,030 --> 00:39:31,510
you can't really check that I don't know web sockets has been used correctly for all request handling.

655
00:39:31,510 --> 00:39:35,589
you just can't check that with a simple static test.

656
00:39:35,589 --> 00:39:39,510
which in that case argued is based on reflection and using annotations in the code.

657
00:39:39,510 --> 00:39:41,030
to spot different types of component.

658
00:39:41,040 --> 00:39:45,430
they could be useful but definitely insufficient, it's only part of the problem definitely.

659
00:39:45,440 --> 00:39:51,750
Yeah, let's talk about adoption of a continuous architecture approach in a company.

660
00:39:51,750 --> 00:39:56,470
where somebody says you know what we need to adopt this approach to architecture.

661
00:39:56,470 --> 00:39:58,711
because we think is is a good one. Yeah, can give some benefit.

662
00:39:58,720 --> 00:40:06,230
Now my first question about this is are there any specific organizational structures.

663
00:40:06,230 --> 00:40:10,550
or cultures that for which continuous architecture is particularly challenging to adopt?

664
00:40:10,550 --> 00:40:15,750
I think it's very difficult to adopt where there's a centralization of architecture and decision-m.

665
00:40:15,750 --> 00:40:19,030
and so you you have a very entrenched team of they're probably called solution architects.

666
00:40:19,030 --> 00:40:20,630
or maybe called software architects.

667
00:40:20,630 --> 00:40:25,270
and they sort of work across the teams telling the teams what their decisions are.

668
00:40:25,280 --> 00:40:33,589
Sometimes you can be pleasantly surprised and those teams react very positively to a more decentralized bottomup approach.

669
00:40:33,589 --> 00:40:39,430
but frequently if they find it quite challenging they will advise senior technology management that chaos will ensue.

670
00:40:39,430 --> 00:40:42,710
there will be no governance and the bad things will happen.

671
00:40:42,710 --> 00:40:46,870
Um, and so you have to work to counter the fears because some of the fears are legitimate.

672
00:40:46,870 --> 00:40:48,630
and explain how you're not trying to achieve anarchy.

673
00:40:48,640 --> 00:40:50,470
You're trying to empower teams.

674
00:40:50,470 --> 00:40:54,069
They're two different outcomes and how you can empower teams without it being an.

675
00:40:54,069 --> 00:40:56,230
but it does require a certain change of mindset.

676
00:40:56,240 --> 00:41:02,870
